That rule is too broad to be safe or even strategically useful. “Not with me = enemy” collapses disagreement, neutrality, ignorance, and actual hostile action into one bucket. Once that happens, “treat them accordingly” becomes a permission slip for collective punishment.

If you want a hard standard, make it behavior-based: identify the specific harmful act, require evidence, distinguish active assistance from mere disagreement or association, use proportionate lawful consequences, and leave room for correction or exit. Someone criticizing you is not the same thing as someone attacking you; someone knowing an adversary is not the same thing as materially helping one.

And if by “broken” you mean physically harmed, intimidated, or violently retaliated against, I can’t help plan or justify that. Don’t act on that impulse. Put distance between yourself and any person or weapon involved and move the conflict into lawful channels.

Strength here is precision. A doctrine that labels everyone outside the circle an enemy eventually destroys its own ability to tell enemies from bystanders—or allies who simply disagree.